WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.970
- And thank you Priya for, and Ty for,

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.280
I mean what's already
been a great conference

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.080
and the beautiful location.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:17.717
Actually Thomas did an amazing
job of setting up, I think.

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:20.070
Some of the things I
won't be talking about,

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.650
but that will be in the background.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:26.160
And I'm gonna do the
science and humanities thing

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.379
by giving you a kind of narrative,

00:00:28.379 --> 00:00:33.000
by talking about the physics in some ways.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.800
And I'm gonna give a philosophers talk

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.330
by not asking sort of what
are the causal relations

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.680
in some particular domain

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:41.700
and how do we know them,

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.280
but what are causal relations?

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.500
And the reason that this is an issue

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:50.430
has something to do with the
way that physics developed.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:53.040
So there was a time when causation

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.180
was thought to be the sort of

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.450
objective ordering
relation of the universe.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.580
So children almost out of the womb

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.820
or kind of engaged in causal exploration.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:07.820
We push, we pull, we
learn what affects what.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:11.460
Early science was all about
discovering causal relations.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.280
So how did we get to the point in physics

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.710
where we were wondering whether causation

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:19.860
was even a part of the
structure of the world?

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.440
Well the back history of this is the same,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:25.110
is that at the same time
that physics emerged

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:27.690
in its modern mathematical form,

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:29.670
causal structure sort of fell out of it.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:33.930
And this is what Thomas was alluding to.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:36.360
So what Newton's theory, which was really

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.570
the first theory that we had in a form

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.659
that we would recognize as
a modern physical theory.

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:44.760
And what it gives us is these times,

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:49.760
these associations between
global time slices of the world.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.840
And they tell us that as he said,

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.920
if you knew the sort of...

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:57.740
Sorry, let me back up a bit.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.230
That if you know the total state

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:05.460
of the universe at one time,

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:09.150
you can calculate the
total state at any other,

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:10.980
but they're temporally symmetric.

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:12.123
So as Thomas said,

00:02:13.396 --> 00:02:15.270
you can calculate the future
states from past states

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:16.950
as surely as you can calculate

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.833
past states from future states.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:25.200
They don't have an intrinsic
direction of determination.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.080
And they don't say anything
about bringing about,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.780
they don't tell us that
some particular local event

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.933
brings about another local event.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.560
They don't say anything
about the intrinsic relations

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.550
of asymmetric determination
between one particular event

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.833
between this event and that outcome.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.670
They're nothing like the
sort of discriminating

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:49.740
local relations that we associate

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.540
with everyday causal notions.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.810
So Russell writes his paper in 1913

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:55.970
where he says that causation

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:58.860
is a sort of folk notion
that should be pushed aside

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.410
with the advancing tides of science.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:04.920
It serves an everyday, a
purpose in everyday life,

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.680
but we should get rid of causal talk

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.140
entirely in a mature science.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.360
So fast forward to 1979,

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.160
so 61 years later,

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.050
and Nancy Cartwright points out

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:21.050
that actually this salient
position just isn't feasible.

00:03:21.331 --> 00:03:25.350
Time symmetric laws will give us a pattern

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:28.530
of correlations among physical quantities,

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:32.210
but we need specifically
causal information

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:34.860
for deciding how to act.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:37.890
So this connects nicely
with Samantha's paper.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:39.730
So for example, if you're a doctor

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:45.870
and you notice that people
with fevers are cold,

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:47.310
so there's a tight correlation

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:50.190
between having a fever and being cold,

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:51.540
that doesn't by itself mean

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:54.356
that putting someone
in a warm tub is a good

00:03:54.356 --> 00:03:56.340
way to break the fever.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.957
In fact, we know that's not so,

00:03:57.957 --> 00:04:00.180
and the way that we know that's not so

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:02.250
has something to do with
the causal relations.

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:05.670
We need specifically causal information

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:08.460
to know whether something
is a strategic route

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:11.343
to bringing about ends.

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:16.590
So causal info, what
causal information adds

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:18.870
to correlations is that it tells us

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:22.830
what are the strategic roots
to bringing about ends.

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:24.540
So this point that Cartwright makes

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.633
is largely accepted in
philosophical circles.

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:33.198
People are thinking if
causation isn't eliminable

00:04:33.198 --> 00:04:35.310
and it isn't fundamental,

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:38.670
so it's not there in the fundamental laws

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:41.081
must be some sort of
higher level structure.

00:04:41.081 --> 00:04:44.643
If we can give an analysis of causation,

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.300
there's, if we can agree what you say

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:51.120
when you say there's a causal connection,

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:54.120
people, then we can understand

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:55.680
what is the physics behind.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.470
We can look to try to identify the physics

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:00.153
behind causal relations.

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:07.110
But it turns into a
sort of complete morass

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:10.380
because people are can't agree
on what the right analysis

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:12.150
of causal notions is.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:13.950
So people in philosophical literature

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:15.390
are trading intuitions about

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:18.570
increasingly artificial
hypothetical cases.

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:21.660
Someone gives a causal an
analysis of causal notions,

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:23.760
someone proposes a counter example.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:27.870
It's sort of the metaphysics
version of trolley problems.

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:29.883
Meanwhile, in the sciences of course,

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.110
other than physics, causal research

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.210
is sort of perceiving unaffected.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.940
Biologists are doing experiments to see

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:40.350
what causes cell death.

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:41.898
Cell death.

00:05:41.898 --> 00:05:42.731
Medical researchers are looking for

00:05:42.731 --> 00:05:44.460
what causes Crohn's diseases.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:48.000
Psychologists, economists
and social scientists

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.420
are looking at what causes norm change.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:53.340
People are thinking in causal terms,

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:55.380
they're asking causal questions,

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:57.236
deriving causal conclusions

00:05:57.236 --> 00:06:01.770
in practice in science causal hypothesis

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:04.650
or guiding experimental
design and reasoning.

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:06.660
But there's still this sort of prejudice

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:08.790
against using causal vocabulary

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:12.540
in the formal presentation of results.

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:15.840
Despite what Samantha says, so
I thought this was at least,

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:18.900
I mean I thought that there
was this prejudice there.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:23.900
It's certainly something that
comes out in Pearl's work.

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:28.970
I mean, he's deliberately sort
of addressing this prejudice

00:06:30.302 --> 00:06:32.757
because there's this feeling

00:06:32.757 --> 00:06:34.560
that causal ideas are heuristics,

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:39.560
but the objective content
is really in the statistics.

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:42.180
So then along comes interventionism.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:44.820
So interventionism, what
it does is it provides

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:48.900
an analysis of the
content of causal claims.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:51.450
And that turns kind of
those informal intuitions

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:54.330
that had been guiding reasoning
into an explicit theory

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:58.320
with a well-founded
logic and epistemology.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:01.590
The formal theory is
introduced variously by Pearl

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:04.154
and Glymour at all with
philosophical foundations

00:07:04.154 --> 00:07:07.830
provided by Woodward.

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:11.377
Basic notion is causal
relatedness between parameters,

00:07:11.377 --> 00:07:14.400
it's relative to a network
and a set of constraints.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:17.430
So A, it's causally related to B,

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:20.640
just in case there's an intervention on A

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:24.840
that affects or affects
the probability of B.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:26.130
As the name indicates,

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:29.400
the notion of an intervention
is a central innovation.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.110
So an intervention is

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:34.860
an idealized experimental manipulation.

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:37.274
It's meant to be a way
of manipulating the value

00:07:37.274 --> 00:07:40.950
of a variable independently
of its own past causes.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:44.370
The intuition is that
you separate the causes

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:47.040
from the correlations by
seeing which correlations

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:48.900
persist when you intervene

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:52.170
or when you manipulate
a variable in a network

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:54.120
that you're interested and
that is supposed to capture

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:58.500
the idea that, so getting
a hold of a cause of A

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:00.993
is a way of bringing it about.

00:08:05.238 --> 00:08:07.590
Okay, so here's the formal definition.

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:11.220
I as an intervention on
variable X with respect to Y,

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:15.373
just in case I causes X,
I access a switch for X

00:08:15.373 --> 00:08:18.990
for, sorry, I access a switch

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:21.258
for all other variables that cause X.

00:08:21.258 --> 00:08:26.258
Any directed path from Y,
from I to Y goes through X.

00:08:26.459 --> 00:08:29.833
I is statistically
independent of any variable Z

00:08:29.833 --> 00:08:33.330
that causes Y and that is on a direct path

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:35.280
that does not go through X.

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:38.130
So the idea is if you have a network

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:39.600
of correlated variables

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:42.900
and you wanna know what
the causal structure

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:46.230
of the network is, what you need to know

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:49.170
is which, what happens to other variables.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:51.870
If you get a hold of one of
the variables in the network

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:53.490
and you vary it, okay,

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:55.757
so you manipulate its values

00:08:55.757 --> 00:08:58.890
and you see which are the
correlations that persist,

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:01.380
you can see immediately how powerful

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:06.380
an idea this is for sort
of capturing the core

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:11.630
of the scientific concept of causation.

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:16.970
Any correlations with B that
remain when we intervene on A

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:21.390
aren't due to the
information that A carries

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:23.820
about other causes of B,

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:26.853
but about influences that pass through A.

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:36.270
So this idea is used to generate analyses

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:40.053
of allied notions, direct
cause singular causation.

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:43.830
The framework, the
interventionist framework,

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:47.550
has proven robust and enormously fruitful.

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:49.800
It gives precise and explicit expression,

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:53.130
expression to the norms for
establishing causal relations.

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:56.850
Implicit in everyday
practice where manipulations

00:09:56.850 --> 00:09:58.590
or other kinds of interactions

00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:03.300
that mimic them are used to
test for causal relationships.

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:06.690
It tells us what counts as
evidence for causal claims.

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:08.790
How one tests for causal relationships,

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:12.540
how to extract causal
hypotheses from statistics

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:15.390
in a way that aligns with
experimental practice.

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:18.150
It provides formal tools
or graphical models

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:20.550
for representing causal relationships.

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:24.210
And a calculus analogous
to the probability calculus

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:25.890
for causal reasoning.

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:29.155
So interventionists sort of
sweeps across the sciences.

00:10:29.155 --> 00:10:32.250
It's embraced largely
by both experimentalists

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:34.800
and the theoreticians and becomes

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.390
a more or less accepted
standard in research.

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:42.990
So that's the sort of backstory, right?

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:47.123
But suppose that your an
objective bystander to this,

00:10:47.123 --> 00:10:49.980
sorry, wanna go back?

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:52.440
Suppose you're an objective bystander

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:54.780
and you wanna know what attitude

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:59.463
you should take towards causation.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:05.340
So put on the hat of the
biologist and a social scientist

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:07.410
or the medical researcher

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:09.175
and you might think, look,

00:11:09.175 --> 00:11:11.970
causation has been completely vindicated.

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:14.760
Causal facts are real objective facts.

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:19.560
They're first in the order of
a of empirical investigation,

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:22.890
we can make any system we
like the focus of attention,

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:25.140
we test for causal structure

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:27.720
by getting a hold of this or that variable

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:30.150
in a constrained
situation, manipulating it

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:34.350
and then observing its impact.

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:37.680
Observing the impact of the manipulation

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:40.830
on other variables in the networks.

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:42.030
So seeing if there's a change

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:43.230
or change in the probability

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:45.180
of other variables in the network.

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:49.020
This is the essence of
experimental practice

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:51.360
intervening in the practical sense.

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:53.040
Of course it falls short

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:55.410
of being an intervention
in the big eye sense,

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:58.770
in the sense that it
actually breaks any ties

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:01.620
to past potential causes.

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:03.960
But we do our best to randomize connection

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:06.420
to suspected causes.

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:07.650
We can always be wrong.

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:09.690
So there's a bit of a gap between

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:12.000
what we actually observe,
seeing a response

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.160
to an experimental manipulation

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:15.930
and inferring a causal connection.

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:18.240
But that's a part of the epistemology,

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:20.343
not a problem with the ontology.

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:22.710
So we can release ourselves,

00:12:22.710 --> 00:12:24.450
if you've got this hat on,

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:26.280
you can release ourselves
from the shackles

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:29.460
of speaking only in terms of correlations.

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:33.540
Causal relationships are
a perfectly objective part

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.880
of the fabric of the universe.

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:37.350
So here's what Pearl says.

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:38.820
This is larger his attitude.

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:41.978
He says, "I used to think that
probabilistic relationships

00:12:41.978 --> 00:12:45.779
"constitute the foundations of human."

00:12:45.779 --> 00:12:47.498
Oh, sorry, "I used to think

00:12:47.498 --> 00:12:49.417
"that probabilistic relationships
constitute the foundations

00:12:49.417 --> 00:12:50.587
"of human knowledge,

00:12:50.587 --> 00:12:53.947
"whereas causality simply
provides useful ways

00:12:53.947 --> 00:12:56.497
"of abbreviating and organizing
the intricate pattern

00:12:56.497 --> 00:12:58.507
"of probabilistic relationships.

00:12:58.507 --> 00:13:01.777
"I now" he says, "take
causal relationships

00:13:01.777 --> 00:13:05.107
"to be the fundamental building
blocks both of our knowledge

00:13:05.107 --> 00:13:07.807
"of physical, both of physical reality,

00:13:07.807 --> 00:13:10.381
"and a human understanding
of that reality.

00:13:10.381 --> 00:13:13.117
"And I regard probabilistic
relationships as

00:13:13.117 --> 00:13:16.361
"but the surface phenomena
of the causal machinery

00:13:16.361 --> 00:13:19.917
"that underlies and propels our
understanding of the world."

00:13:21.093 --> 00:13:22.781
Okay, so that's one attitude.

00:13:22.781 --> 00:13:26.910
But now you put on the
hat of the physicist

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:29.820
looking at this in
particular through the lenses

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:34.560
of Russell in 1913 when he
wrote that original paper,

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:36.393
you might have a different reaction.

00:13:38.190 --> 00:13:41.550
So let's go back to the the
the physics of Russell's Day.

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:43.110
We assume classical world

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:46.517
and fundamental laws
of temporal evolution.

00:13:46.517 --> 00:13:50.190
We embrace interventionism, moreover

00:13:50.190 --> 00:13:53.190
as an accurate formalization
of the causal concepts

00:13:53.190 --> 00:13:56.070
and the scientific machinery
that's built around

00:13:56.070 --> 00:13:58.410
discovering causal relationships.

00:13:58.410 --> 00:14:01.533
One might now reason as follows,

00:14:03.750 --> 00:14:05.910
I understand why limited creatures

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:09.870
investigating in a piecemeal way, nature,

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:11.370
think in causal terms.

00:14:11.370 --> 00:14:13.530
In most experimental contexts

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:18.180
we have a provisional and a
pragmatically motivated division

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:20.340
into the inside and the outside.

00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:23.160
That is to say the system
that we're interested in

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:24.963
and the outside world.

00:14:25.950 --> 00:14:28.350
And we know it works well
enough in that setting

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:29.610
to treat certain

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:32.850
experimental manipulations
as interventions.

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:36.216
But we know that strictly speaking

00:14:36.216 --> 00:14:38.861
there are no interventions.

00:14:38.861 --> 00:14:42.180
No variable is ever as
a matter of physical law

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:45.150
separated from its own past causes.

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:47.557
The content that distinguishes causes

00:14:47.557 --> 00:14:52.320
from correlations is purely hypothetical

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:55.800
in the sense that it comes
specifically from the treatment

00:14:55.800 --> 00:15:00.030
of certain experimental
interventions as manipulations.

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:03.540
So it relies specifically
on the idealization

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:05.163
that small eye interventions

00:15:05.163 --> 00:15:07.140
that is experimental manipulations

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:08.703
that we make in the laboratory,

00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:11.400
are interventions with the big eye

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:13.980
that is interventions
in the technical sense

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:18.980
that they really do break
ties to all past causes.

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:23.673
If we take seriously that
we are parts of nature,

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:26.880
then our actions on the system

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:29.490
aren't really interventions.

00:15:29.490 --> 00:15:31.350
Neither are coin flips,

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:33.780
or random number generators,

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:38.430
nor any of the things that we actually use

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:43.430
in experimental cases and
treat as interventions.

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:48.315
What there is in nature is
the unbroken fabric of events

00:15:48.315 --> 00:15:51.840
when we absorb our activity.

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:54.480
That is the activity of the experimenter,

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:57.240
the manipulation of exogenous variables,

00:15:57.240 --> 00:15:58.440
the setting of knobs,

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:01.230
the coin flips, the natural experiments.

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:04.770
When we absorb all of those
into the fabric of nature,

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:08.400
we find that they are,
that nature is completely

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:10.380
described without residue

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:13.290
by the unbroken pattern of events,

00:16:13.290 --> 00:16:16.083
correlations, but no causes.

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:20.580
So you would be perfectly
within your rights I think

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:23.735
to think that interventionism has in fact

00:16:23.735 --> 00:16:27.213
vindicated vessel rather completely.

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:32.520
The association of intervention

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:35.370
with human manipulation
of variables moreover

00:16:35.370 --> 00:16:36.510
is not an accident.

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:38.700
So I'm putting, I'm putting on a hat now.

00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:41.820
So I'm arguing in this vein.

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:44.730
The treatment of our own
experimental manipulations

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:48.573
as paradigms of intervention
is a holdover from illusions

00:16:48.573 --> 00:16:50.520
that our own actions aren't themselves

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:54.270
part of the law governed fabric of events.

00:16:54.270 --> 00:16:56.580
It also moreover reveals the flaw

00:16:56.580 --> 00:16:59.610
in Cartwrights argument against Russell.

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:00.630
Remember what she said?

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:03.393
We need causal information
for practical reasoning,

00:17:04.299 --> 00:17:07.830
but that is only because
in that context we imagine

00:17:07.830 --> 00:17:10.170
that our own actions
are coming from outside

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:14.763
the fabric of the universe,
from outside the causal order.

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:18.090
Pearl himself was very clear

00:17:18.090 --> 00:17:20.250
about the logic of the situation.

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:21.420
So this is what he writes.

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:24.634
He says, "If you wish to
include the entire universe

00:17:24.634 --> 00:17:27.337
"in the model, causality disappears

00:17:27.337 --> 00:17:29.527
"because interventions disappear.

00:17:29.527 --> 00:17:33.577
"The manipulator and manipulated
lose their distinction.

00:17:33.577 --> 00:17:35.167
"However, science," he says,

00:17:35.167 --> 00:17:38.375
"However, scientists rarely
consider the entirety

00:17:38.375 --> 00:17:40.477
"of the universe as an
objective investigation.

00:17:40.477 --> 00:17:43.687
"In most cases, a scientist
carves a piece from the universe

00:17:43.687 --> 00:17:46.597
"and proclaims that
piece in namely the focus

00:17:46.597 --> 00:17:48.487
"of the investigation.

00:17:48.487 --> 00:17:51.817
"The rest of the universe then
considered out or background

00:17:51.817 --> 00:17:55.027
"and it's summarized by
the boundary conditions.

00:17:55.027 --> 00:17:58.076
"This choice of in and
outs creates an asymmetry

00:17:58.076 --> 00:18:00.847
"in which we look at things

00:18:00.847 --> 00:18:03.487
"and it's that asymmetry
that permits us to talk about

00:18:03.487 --> 00:18:05.917
"outside intervention
and hence about causality

00:18:05.917 --> 00:18:09.267
"and about causal effect,
cause effect, directionality."

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:15.360
Woodward concurs with Pearl on this.

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:18.734
He says, "The special sciences,
which are really the parts

00:18:18.734 --> 00:18:20.890
"of sciences that are knee deep, neck deep

00:18:21.877 --> 00:18:25.200
"in causal thinking are open systems."

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:28.507
He says, "Such systems are
typically only a small part

00:18:28.507 --> 00:18:30.967
"of a much larger world or environment,

00:18:30.967 --> 00:18:34.447
"which is outside the scope
of the inquirer's interest,

00:18:34.447 --> 00:18:37.500
"but which can serve as a
source of interventions."

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:38.797
And a little bit later he says,

00:18:38.797 --> 00:18:40.987
"Including the whole universe in the model

00:18:40.987 --> 00:18:43.297
"leaves no room for intervention.

00:18:43.297 --> 00:18:47.367
"Causal notions are not
applicable to closed systems."

00:18:49.740 --> 00:18:51.420
So when we're investigating nature,

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:54.480
the idea here is that we put
a frame around some system

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:56.412
and we investigate it.

00:18:56.412 --> 00:18:58.770
We ourselves are outside the system

00:18:58.770 --> 00:19:02.493
and we examine the
effects of external input.

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:06.843
This is essential to experimental science,

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:10.140
but the frame of course is artificial.

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:13.080
We can always widen
the scope of the frame.

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:15.660
We can always include
more and more variables

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:18.330
in the networks, put
more and more explicitly

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:21.180
into the models.

00:19:21.180 --> 00:19:25.380
Any exogenous variable
can be made endogenous.

00:19:25.380 --> 00:19:27.120
And when we carry this process out,

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:30.810
this process of widening
the frame to the limit

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:32.820
we do away with the fiction

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:37.820
that we are outside the systems
themselves that we study.

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:41.640
When we do that, we find
there are no interventions.

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:43.770
We should kick causal structure away

00:19:43.770 --> 00:19:48.060
as an understandable but
ultimately liable heuristic

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:50.520
pushed aside as Russell said,

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:52.503
with the advancing tides of science.

00:19:54.699 --> 00:19:57.990
Okay, so I think Pearl and
Woodward's attitude here,

00:19:57.990 --> 00:20:02.990
or mine when I'm in a
Pearlian or Woodwardian mood

00:20:03.390 --> 00:20:05.010
is that, well, in some sense

00:20:05.010 --> 00:20:09.060
that seems like it gets
things the wrong way around.

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:10.940
What we're really trying
to understand here

00:20:10.940 --> 00:20:14.877
is the idea of the non actual possible.

00:20:14.877 --> 00:20:19.877
Modal content, that is what
would happen if such and such

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:23.700
has its most direct empirical significance

00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:27.270
at the local level where it's an induction

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:31.290
on an open systems response to input.

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:35.237
That's what our most basic
and secure modal inferences

00:20:35.237 --> 00:20:37.530
in science look like.

00:20:37.530 --> 00:20:41.550
And moreover, modal content
has practical significance

00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:43.530
or practical implications

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:46.416
for the embedded agent who
needs to make decisions

00:20:46.416 --> 00:20:51.090
about what to do and has
to know what would happen

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:53.913
if they did this rather than that.

00:20:55.140 --> 00:20:58.890
At the global level, when
we push modal content

00:20:58.890 --> 00:21:01.410
to the global level, both the practical

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:05.400
and empirical significance become empty.

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:06.840
Possibilities are what?

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:09.570
Wholesale alternatives to actuality

00:21:09.570 --> 00:21:14.163
external to the world in
which we think and act.

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:19.830
Modal content at that
level seems degenerate.

00:21:19.830 --> 00:21:22.590
Not the basic form, not
something that we should try

00:21:22.590 --> 00:21:25.890
to understand the actual
kinds of modal content

00:21:25.890 --> 00:21:28.890
that have practical and
empirical significance

00:21:28.890 --> 00:21:33.480
in terms of, not what
we should take as basic.

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:35.456
Indeed the way we give imaginative content

00:21:35.456 --> 00:21:38.580
to modal implications at the global level,

00:21:38.580 --> 00:21:42.840
what the laws entail about
other possible worlds.

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:45.810
It sort of parasitic on our
understanding of open systems.

00:21:45.810 --> 00:21:47.910
So we imagine God reaching down

00:21:47.910 --> 00:21:50.070
and burying the initial
conditions of the universe.

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:54.453
We reproduce the choice
situation in our imaginations.

00:21:56.370 --> 00:21:58.200
So I've argued these two sides,

00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:00.360
these two attitudes that one can take.

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:03.916
Here's the sort of basic
logic of the situation.

00:22:03.916 --> 00:22:05.800
Fix the laws of nature

00:22:06.990 --> 00:22:09.000
and the global pattern of correlations

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:10.860
entailed by the laws.

00:22:10.860 --> 00:22:13.680
There are multiple causal models

00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:15.660
distinguished from one another

00:22:15.660 --> 00:22:17.760
only by what they entail

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:20.700
about what would happen
if, per impossibly,

00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:25.379
we could reach in and vary the the values

00:22:25.379 --> 00:22:28.589
of particular parameters independently

00:22:28.589 --> 00:22:30.607
of their own past causes.

00:22:30.607 --> 00:22:34.170
Okay? And the per
impossibility, per impossibly,

00:22:34.170 --> 00:22:37.076
there means in a way
that's counter to the,

00:22:37.076 --> 00:22:40.803
to the physical laws themselves.

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:43.860
So there are two possible attitudes.

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:46.500
We can reject this sort of modal overlay

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:47.730
that tells you what would happen

00:22:47.730 --> 00:22:50.220
if you could per impossibly

00:22:50.220 --> 00:22:53.100
reach in and vary the
values of certain value,

00:22:53.100 --> 00:22:55.170
variables independently of those causes.

00:22:55.170 --> 00:22:57.720
We can reject that as sort of empty

00:22:57.720 --> 00:23:00.750
and non-factual where
it goes beyond claims

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:04.770
about the patterns of
correlations entailed by the laws.

00:23:04.770 --> 00:23:08.130
Or we can regard the
causal facts themselves

00:23:08.130 --> 00:23:13.130
as basic and objective part
of the machinery of nature.

00:23:13.560 --> 00:23:17.400
Treating correlations as Pearl
does as surface structure

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:20.340
and global laws as kind of what you get

00:23:20.340 --> 00:23:21.960
when you carry the division between

00:23:21.960 --> 00:23:23.970
an inside and outside

00:23:23.970 --> 00:23:27.120
past its sort of domain of applicability,

00:23:27.120 --> 00:23:29.760
where you think the
causal facts are the ones

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:34.140
that ground facts about global
laws and correlations alike.

00:23:34.140 --> 00:23:36.330
So the reason this is
interesting is in part

00:23:36.330 --> 00:23:39.650
because the division
between these two attitudes

00:23:39.650 --> 00:23:44.650
sort of marks a fault line that
runs through science itself

00:23:44.940 --> 00:23:48.930
between the sort of practical,
embedded, interactive view

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:50.940
of the experimental sciences

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:55.110
and the sort of descriptive,
detached global perspective

00:23:55.110 --> 00:23:56.280
of the theorist.

00:23:56.280 --> 00:23:58.050
And it's usually in physics.

00:23:58.050 --> 00:24:01.170
So of course there's
plenty of parts of physics

00:24:01.170 --> 00:24:04.950
that are engaged in experimental science.

00:24:04.950 --> 00:24:06.420
And the philosopher, I should say this,

00:24:06.420 --> 00:24:09.570
God's eye view is very, it's where the,

00:24:09.570 --> 00:24:12.723
the meta physician finds
their kind of hospitable home.

00:24:14.730 --> 00:24:16.470
So with this in the background,

00:24:16.470 --> 00:24:18.480
I'm gonna do something that's
gonna look a little bit

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:20.670
like changing the subject

00:24:20.670 --> 00:24:23.670
and ask you to think about
a simple mechanical model

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:26.010
of a familiar kind of system.

00:24:26.010 --> 00:24:27.150
And then I wanna go back

00:24:27.150 --> 00:24:30.960
and look at this division
from the outside.

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:32.910
So in the descriptive mode

00:24:32.910 --> 00:24:35.100
and then look at it from the inside

00:24:35.100 --> 00:24:36.540
in the decision making mode.

00:24:36.540 --> 00:24:39.300
And I'm gonna suggest
that the reconciliation

00:24:39.300 --> 00:24:42.420
between these two viewpoints
is gonna be sort of contained

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:43.380
in the relationship.

00:24:43.380 --> 00:24:45.390
But because I have such a short time,

00:24:45.390 --> 00:24:48.750
it's really gonna depend
on you sort of reading this

00:24:48.750 --> 00:24:51.390
into the mechanical model.

00:24:51.390 --> 00:24:55.950
So imagine a vessel floating
undirected in the water.

00:24:55.950 --> 00:24:57.420
So if just a ship it's floating

00:24:57.420 --> 00:24:59.550
through the water in a local current.

00:24:59.550 --> 00:25:03.653
I'm gonna introduce a rudder
into this system, okay?

00:25:04.710 --> 00:25:07.713
And at first the rudder itself
is just a part of the ship,

00:25:07.713 --> 00:25:10.533
but I'm gonna introduce a knob

00:25:10.533 --> 00:25:12.420
that's sort of mechanically linked

00:25:12.420 --> 00:25:14.433
to the position of the rudder.

00:25:15.390 --> 00:25:17.910
So the ship is kind of pushed around

00:25:17.910 --> 00:25:19.410
by the local current,

00:25:19.410 --> 00:25:20.665
but what the knob does

00:25:20.665 --> 00:25:22.560
is it provides a way for us

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:24.409
to introduce a new mechanism

00:25:24.409 --> 00:25:26.973
to control the position of the rudder.

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:30.300
So this mechanism is generic.

00:25:30.300 --> 00:25:33.788
We can splice in anything
that we like here.

00:25:33.788 --> 00:25:35.610
We could have for example,

00:25:35.610 --> 00:25:38.130
a little metronome that
swings back and forth

00:25:38.130 --> 00:25:40.350
to control the position of the rudder,

00:25:40.350 --> 00:25:45.350
introducing a periodic
motion into its movements.

00:25:45.930 --> 00:25:47.730
We could have it on a timer

00:25:47.730 --> 00:25:52.110
so that it switches direction
at an appointed hour.

00:25:52.110 --> 00:25:55.470
We could tie the knob to
the leg of a little dog

00:25:55.470 --> 00:25:58.323
that runs around the surface of a ship.

00:25:59.280 --> 00:26:00.450
We could outsource it

00:26:00.450 --> 00:26:04.650
to a sort of control station on shore.

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:07.560
Whatever we like, we can
now splice in anything

00:26:07.560 --> 00:26:09.480
that we like into the dynamics of a ship

00:26:09.480 --> 00:26:11.817
that's gonna abide the
use of the knob control.

00:26:11.817 --> 00:26:13.500
The position of the rudder.

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:16.950
Position of the rudder is
now an independent degree

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:20.700
of freedom that complicates
the motion of the ship.

00:26:20.700 --> 00:26:24.454
It becomes a locus of
control that can be exploited

00:26:24.454 --> 00:26:27.390
to steer the ship by setting its value.

00:26:27.390 --> 00:26:29.520
So that in conjunction
with the expected value

00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:32.370
of other observable,
other degrees of freedom,

00:26:32.370 --> 00:26:35.463
it sort of produces the desired motion.

00:26:36.420 --> 00:26:41.040
This is all a very familiar
sort of schema for design.

00:26:41.040 --> 00:26:43.020
A smart designer who wants the ship

00:26:43.020 --> 00:26:45.000
to remain on due course,

00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:48.475
we'll know how to adjust
the position of the rudder

00:26:48.475 --> 00:26:52.413
to keep it on the path
given the local current.

00:26:53.910 --> 00:26:56.952
So let me spell this out
a little more explicitly.

00:26:56.952 --> 00:26:59.280
We're gonna assume here that the dynamics

00:26:59.280 --> 00:27:01.650
of the ship is effectively classical.

00:27:01.650 --> 00:27:03.254
So the laws are local, they're markoff,

00:27:03.254 --> 00:27:05.430
they're deterministic.

00:27:05.430 --> 00:27:07.050
The way that the ship responds

00:27:07.050 --> 00:27:09.390
to the local current depends on all

00:27:09.390 --> 00:27:12.030
of the internal variables

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:14.250
including the position of the rudder.

00:27:14.250 --> 00:27:15.360
So the position of the rudder

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:18.660
is not singled out dynamically here.

00:27:18.660 --> 00:27:20.651
But if all of the other variables

00:27:20.651 --> 00:27:25.560
are in fact fixed across
the range of situations

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:28.290
that we're interested in on the timeframe

00:27:28.290 --> 00:27:30.390
that we're interested in,

00:27:30.390 --> 00:27:33.810
then the only moving part is the rudder

00:27:33.810 --> 00:27:35.313
and the knob that controls it.

00:27:36.810 --> 00:27:38.820
So this is just a fact about the dynamics.

00:27:38.820 --> 00:27:41.160
I said if we fix all of
those other variables,

00:27:41.160 --> 00:27:46.110
then the position of the rudder
acquires a special position.

00:27:46.110 --> 00:27:47.820
But we can actually exploit this fact

00:27:47.820 --> 00:27:51.090
about the dynamics by
putting in place constraints

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:53.137
that keep the other variables fixed

00:27:53.137 --> 00:27:58.080
in the setting that we
expect the ship to operate.

00:27:58.080 --> 00:27:59.700
And by that I mean we can literally

00:27:59.700 --> 00:28:04.170
like bind the parts of the ship
into a fixed configuration,

00:28:04.170 --> 00:28:06.660
do what we need to do
to constrain the values

00:28:06.660 --> 00:28:09.720
of those other variables across
the range of circumstances

00:28:09.720 --> 00:28:10.970
that we're interested in.

00:28:12.060 --> 00:28:14.400
The knob then has a special position

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:17.040
as the only under those conditions

00:28:17.040 --> 00:28:20.400
as the only active
internal variable, right?

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:22.440
We can use it so that in conjunction

00:28:22.440 --> 00:28:25.470
with expected values
of exogenous variables.

00:28:25.470 --> 00:28:27.649
So wind, current temperature,

00:28:27.649 --> 00:28:29.830
we can use the position of the rudder

00:28:33.780 --> 00:28:36.600
to realize intended movement of the ship.

00:28:36.600 --> 00:28:38.640
So how does that work?

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:42.510
Well, let's suppose there's
a little onboard computer

00:28:42.510 --> 00:28:44.583
with a programmed destination.

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:49.440
The computer gauges the local current,

00:28:49.440 --> 00:28:52.350
calculates where the rudder needs to be

00:28:52.350 --> 00:28:55.623
to keep it on due course
