00:43:05.490 --> 00:43:06.930
So you say what they're,

00:43:06.930 --> 00:43:08.880
what I believe in is fundamental physics,

00:43:08.880 --> 00:43:10.590
and I'm trying to find a place in a world

00:43:10.590 --> 00:43:12.570
that is fundamentally determined

00:43:12.570 --> 00:43:15.300
by physical laws for causation.

00:43:15.300 --> 00:43:16.980
So if you're starting
from the point of view

00:43:16.980 --> 00:43:19.627
where you say, "Look, I understand

00:43:19.627 --> 00:43:22.050
"why we reason causally and so on."

00:43:22.050 --> 00:43:26.100
But that's partly because
of contingent limitations

00:43:26.100 --> 00:43:27.750
and it's a convenient heuristic.

00:43:27.750 --> 00:43:30.379
But what there is in the fabric of nature

00:43:30.379 --> 00:43:31.380
is just what the laws tell us.

00:43:31.380 --> 00:43:33.030
So, you know, I haven't given

00:43:33.030 --> 00:43:34.530
an argument for that position.

00:43:34.530 --> 00:43:36.777
It's a starting challenge.

00:43:36.777 --> 00:43:39.153
It was the challenge
that I was starting with.

00:43:40.140 --> 00:43:42.690
So in effect, it's asking for you,

00:43:42.690 --> 00:43:45.340
tell me why I should believe
in causal relationships.

00:43:48.540 --> 00:43:49.750
- [Host] Okay, Tim, quick question.

00:43:49.750 --> 00:43:51.060
- Yeah, this is really quick,

00:43:51.060 --> 00:43:53.100
but it just, it seems to me

00:43:53.100 --> 00:43:54.810
you had on the screen in the middle,

00:43:54.810 --> 00:43:57.397
there're these two attitudes
and that rises out of Russell.

00:43:57.397 --> 00:43:58.320
- Yeah.

00:43:58.320 --> 00:44:00.630
- It seems is just a false dilemma, right?

00:44:00.630 --> 00:44:02.557
The options seem to be, Russell says,

00:44:02.557 --> 00:44:04.567
"I don't see causation
in the laws of physics,

00:44:04.567 --> 00:44:05.730
"so there's no causation."

00:44:05.730 --> 00:44:07.717
And the other one is, "Well, no causation

00:44:07.717 --> 00:44:09.270
"is basic and fundamental."

00:44:09.270 --> 00:44:11.430
You wanna say Russell,
you don't see pianos

00:44:11.430 --> 00:44:13.290
in the laws of physics either.

00:44:13.290 --> 00:44:15.450
Okay? But they're real. Okay.

00:44:15.450 --> 00:44:16.283
They're really real.

00:44:16.283 --> 00:44:17.820
They're not fictive

00:44:17.820 --> 00:44:19.585
and they're not fundamental either, right?

00:44:19.585 --> 00:44:20.418
- No.

00:44:20.418 --> 00:44:21.510
- So there are lots of things in the world

00:44:21.510 --> 00:44:24.420
that are neither written
into the laws of physics

00:44:24.420 --> 00:44:26.857
nor merely fictive.
- Of course.

00:44:26.857 --> 00:44:27.690
- They're all the emerging things,

00:44:27.690 --> 00:44:28.770
tables and chairs and everything.

00:44:28.770 --> 00:44:30.270
So it just seems like a false dilemma

00:44:30.270 --> 00:44:31.560
when you set it up that way.

00:44:31.560 --> 00:44:32.793
Why can't causation be like that?

00:44:32.793 --> 00:44:33.626
- Oh, okay.

00:44:33.626 --> 00:44:34.530
- It's as real as a piano.

00:44:34.530 --> 00:44:35.700
- Yeah, yeah, I understand.

00:44:35.700 --> 00:44:37.440
So this is the way the dialectic went.

00:44:37.440 --> 00:44:39.060
You start by think assuming

00:44:39.060 --> 00:44:40.921
that causal relations are fundamental,

00:44:40.921 --> 00:44:42.383
then you get the physics

00:44:42.383 --> 00:44:44.940
and you don't see them in
the fundamental physics.

00:44:44.940 --> 00:44:47.700
So then the back, I then the step backward

00:44:47.700 --> 00:44:49.890
is, okay, so what are causal relations?

00:44:49.890 --> 00:44:51.390
As soon as we have an analysis,

00:44:51.390 --> 00:44:54.090
then we can understand the
physics that underwrites them.

00:44:54.090 --> 00:44:56.040
People sort of taught, you know,

00:44:56.040 --> 00:44:57.780
cast around for an analysis.

00:44:57.780 --> 00:44:59.185
It takes a long time.

00:44:59.185 --> 00:45:01.230
Interventionism comes along.

00:45:01.230 --> 00:45:04.170
And that seems to, that
seems to coalesce people

00:45:04.170 --> 00:45:06.750
around the idea that this
is the right analysis

00:45:06.750 --> 00:45:08.070
for causal relations.

00:45:08.070 --> 00:45:10.260
So how do we under, this
is the right analysis

00:45:10.260 --> 00:45:11.640
of the content of causal claims.

00:45:11.640 --> 00:45:12.900
What are you saying about the world

00:45:12.900 --> 00:45:14.820
when you say there's causal relationship?

00:45:14.820 --> 00:45:18.185
Analyze in terms of what happens

00:45:18.185 --> 00:45:20.610
under hypothetical interventions.

00:45:20.610 --> 00:45:23.490
And then look, challenge reasserts itself,

00:45:23.490 --> 00:45:26.402
because you say, what is a
hypothetical intervention?

00:45:26.402 --> 00:45:28.290
An intervention is something

00:45:28.290 --> 00:45:30.526
that explicitly seems to go,

00:45:30.526 --> 00:45:34.500
you know, is is counter legal.

00:45:34.500 --> 00:45:37.230
It's counter to what the laws entail.

00:45:37.230 --> 00:45:39.420
So that reopens the position

00:45:39.420 --> 00:45:41.070
for a salient point of view.

00:45:41.070 --> 00:45:44.790
So that's the way that the dialectic went.

00:45:44.790 --> 00:45:47.130
I mean, I, you know, it
went very quickly and it,

00:45:47.130 --> 00:45:50.940
and of course I ignored lots
of positions along the way.

00:45:50.940 --> 00:45:55.127
But I really do think
that this is something

00:45:55.127 --> 00:45:59.790
that to me captures the
heart of the conflict

00:45:59.790 --> 00:46:03.273
between two ways of thinking
about causal relationships.

00:46:04.929 --> 00:46:09.929
- Let's thank Jenann for.
(audience applauding)
 

